    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Osteopenia means a DEXA T-score between -1.0 and -2.5, bone density lower than a young adult's but not low enough to be called osteoporosis, which starts at -2.5 or below.</strong> The label alone does not decide treatment: your 10-year fracture risk on the FRAX calculator does, and most fractures actually happen in people labelled osteopenic.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The bands:</strong> T-score -1.0 or higher is normal, between -1.0 and -2.5 is osteopenia, and -2.5 or lower is osteoporosis. A fragility fracture changes the picture regardless of the number.</li>
        <li><strong>How fast it changes:</strong> in a study of 4,957 women aged 67 and over, mild osteopenia took about 17 years for 10% of women to reach osteoporosis, advanced osteopenia about one year.</li>
        <li><strong>The Indian catch:</strong> in one 2025 Indian study, using an ICMR Indian reference instead of a US white reference cut the share of women labelled osteoporotic from 26.6% to 18%.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> supplements play a small supporting role here. Strength and impact training, calcium and vitamin D adequacy, and medication when FRAX says so do the real work.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

