    <div class="jr-prose">

      <h2 id="bands">What is the difference between osteopenia and osteoporosis?</h2>

      <p>
        Only where your T-score falls. Both describe lower than ideal bone mineral density on a DEXA
        scan; osteoporosis is simply the lower band. The cut-offs come from the World Health
        Organization and are applied to the lowest reading at the spine or hip.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">T-score</th><th scope="col">Label</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">-1.0 or higher</th><td>Normal</td><td>Density within the range of a healthy young adult.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Between -1.0 and -2.5</th><td>Osteopenia (low bone mass)</td><td>Lower than ideal. Fracture risk depends on age and other factors, not on this band alone.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">-2.5 or lower</th><td>Osteoporosis</td><td>Low enough that treatment is usually recommended.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">-2.5 or lower plus a fragility fracture</th><td>Severe osteoporosis</td><td>Treatment strongly recommended.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        A T-score compares you with a healthy 30-year-old of the same sex. A score of -1.8 means your
        density is 1.8 standard deviations below that young-adult average. It is not a percentage, and
        it does not mean your bones are 18% weaker. Women before menopause and men under 50 are read on
        the Z-score instead, which compares you with people your own age.
      </p>

      <p>
        One rule overrides the numbers. If you have already broken a hip or a vertebra from a minor fall
        or with no clear injury, clinicians treat you as having osteoporosis whatever the T-score says.
        That fracture is the strongest predictor of the next one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="fractures">Is osteopenia serious?</h2>

      <p>
        It can be, which is the part people miss. Reviews of fracture data note that most people who
        present with a fragility fracture have bone density in the osteopenic range, not the
        osteoporotic range.
      </p>

      <p>
        That sounds alarming until you see why. Far more people sit in the osteopenia band than in the
        osteoporosis band, so even though each person's risk is lower, the group produces more fractures
        overall. Osteopenia is a range, not a verdict. A 52-year-old at -1.2 and a 74-year-old at -2.4
        are both "osteopenic" and have very different futures.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-stats">
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">17 yrs</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">Time for 10% of women with mild osteopenia to reach osteoporosis</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Gourlay+bone+density+testing+interval+transition+to+osteoporosis+older+women+2012" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">NEJM, 2012</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">~1 yr</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">The same transition for women with advanced osteopenia, close to -2.5</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Gourlay+bone+density+testing+interval+transition+to+osteoporosis+older+women+2012" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">NEJM, 2012</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">26.6% → 18%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">Indian women labelled osteoporotic on a US reference versus an ICMR Indian reference</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=ETHNICA+NHANES+ICMR+reference+osteoporosis+overdiagnosis+India" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Retrospective study, 2025</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        How fast it changes depends on where in the band you start. A 2012 study in the <em>New England
        Journal of Medicine</em> followed 4,957 women aged 67 and over for up to 15 years and estimated
        how long it took 10% of each group to cross into osteoporosis. For normal density and mild
        osteopenia it was about 17 years. For moderate osteopenia, about 4.7 years. For advanced
        osteopenia, close to -2.5, about 1.1 years. That is why the right gap before your next scan
        depends on your number, not on a fixed rule.
      </p>

      <h2 id="frax">Does osteopenia need treatment?</h2>

      <p>
        Not automatically. Whether you need medication is decided by your 10-year fracture probability,
        usually calculated with the FRAX tool, which combines age, sex, weight, previous fractures,
        parental hip fracture, smoking, alcohol, steroid use, rheumatoid arthritis and your hip density.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the United States, the long-standing guidance is to offer medication to postmenopausal women
        and men over 50 with osteopenia when the 10-year probability of a hip fracture is 3% or more, or
        of a major osteoporotic fracture is 20% or more. India has its own FRAX model, but no formal
        Indian treatment threshold has been agreed, partly because thresholds depend on cost and access
        to DEXA. In practice your doctor will use FRAX alongside your history. Medication trials were
        mostly run in people with osteoporosis, so the benefit of drugs for mild osteopenia without other
        risk factors is less certain, which is another reason not to treat the label alone.
      </p>

      <h2 id="india">Why your scanner's database matters in India</h2>

      <p>
        Because a T-score is only as good as the young-adult average it is compared against. Many
        scanners in India compare you with a US reference population. Indian reference data, including
        the ICMR database, often sit lower, so the same scan can carry a more severe label on one setting
        than the other.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2025 retrospective Indian study found that using the US NHANES-III reference significantly
        overdiagnosed both osteoporosis and osteopenia: in women, the share labelled osteoporotic fell
        from about 26.6% to 18% when ICMR data were used. A study of South Asian Indians living in the
        United States found the same direction, with Indian-derived standards classifying more people as
        normal. The effect is not uniform. Some hip comparisons show close agreement between databases,
        and the largest shifts appear at the lumbar spine. The practical step is simple: ask which
        reference database your report used, and keep using the same scanner and setting for follow-up
        scans so changes are real changes.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-to-do">What to do after an osteopenia report</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Ask for your FRAX score.</strong> It turns a band into a number that guides treatment.</li>
        <li><strong>Start loading your bones.</strong> Progressive strength training with some impact is the intervention with the best evidence for raising density, which our guide to <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">how to increase bone density</a> covers in detail.</li>
        <li><strong>Check vitamin D and calcium intake.</strong> A blood test for vitamin D, and an honest look at dietary calcium, which is low in many Indian diets. See <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">vitamin D deficiency symptoms</a> for the thresholds Indian labs use.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduce fall risk.</strong> Most fractures need a fall. Eyesight, footwear, loose rugs and balance training matter as much as density.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeat the scan at a sensible interval,</strong> on the same machine, guided by how close you are to -2.5.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Supplements belong at the end of that list, not the start. Collagen has some bone evidence: one
        randomised trial in 131 postmenopausal women with reduced density found 5g a day of specific
        collagen peptides improved density at the spine and femoral neck against placebo over 12 months.
        That is one trial, of one branded peptide. Skelecoll's 10,000mg of collagen is above that dose,
        and its 250mg of calcium and 600 IU of vitamin D3 are maintenance amounts that contribute to daily
        targets. None of it replaces training, medication when FRAX calls for it, or correcting a measured
        deficiency.
      </p>

