      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the difference between osteopenia and osteoporosis?</summary>
          <p>Only the T-score band. Osteopenia is a T-score between -1.0 and -2.5, and osteoporosis is -2.5 or lower. Both mean bone density below a young adult's average; osteoporosis is the lower band.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is a T-score of -1.5 bad?</summary>
          <p>It is mild osteopenia. In a large study of women aged 67 and over, it took about 17 years for 10% of women with normal density or mild osteopenia to reach osteoporosis. Your FRAX score and history matter more than this number alone.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can osteopenia be reversed?</summary>
          <p>Density can improve, especially with progressive strength and impact training, adequate calcium and vitamin D, and medication where indicated. Even a small rise or a halt in loss reduces risk. The aim is to stop the trend toward osteoporosis rather than to reach a normal score.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does osteopenia need medication?</summary>
          <p>Not automatically. Medication is decided by your 10-year fracture risk on FRAX and by any previous fragility fracture. US guidance treats osteopenia when 10-year hip fracture risk is 3% or more or major fracture risk is 20% or more.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is a FRAX score?</summary>
          <p>FRAX estimates your 10-year probability of a hip fracture and of a major osteoporotic fracture from age, sex, weight, past fractures, family history, smoking, alcohol, steroid use, rheumatoid arthritis and hip density. India has its own FRAX model.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why does my DEXA report say osteoporosis on one machine and osteopenia on another?</summary>
          <p>The reference database may differ. A 2025 Indian study found that using a US reference instead of an ICMR Indian reference raised the share of women labelled osteoporotic from 18% to 26.6%. Ask which database was used and repeat scans on the same machine.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can supplements fix osteopenia?</summary>
          <p>No supplement replaces training, fall prevention and medication when needed. Correcting a measured vitamin D or calcium shortfall helps, and one trial of 5g of specific collagen peptides showed improved spine and hip density, but that is a single study.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

